  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nSurface high pressure will remain overhead through Wednesday\nnight. This will allow for strong radiational cooling tonight\nwith lows into the teens across much of the interior with 20s\nalong the coast. Mainly sunny skies will prevail on Wednesday\nwith lighter winds and high temperatures mainly into the lower \nto middle 40s. Wednesday night will be cold with lows once again\nin the teens and 20s. \n\nLow pressure will be developing east of FL on Wednesday night\nwithin a southern stream s/wv trof before moving northward\ntowards the Carolinas on Thursday while being absorbed by the\nnorthern stream. This low will then cross near New England on \nFriday with a possible secondary coastal low developing over the\nGulf of ME. Thanksgiving itself will feature increasing clouds \nfrom south to north during the day with perhaps some showers \ndeveloping during the early evening across southern locations\nbut otherwise it should be mainly dry with highs into the 40s.\n\nPeriods of moderate to heavy rain will then move from south to\nnorth on Thursday night through at least the first half of\nFriday with rainfall amounts generally ranging between 0.5-1.5\",\nhighest across southern and coastal locations. There remains \nsome potential for pockets of freezing rain and/or sleet on \nThursday evening across some interior/northern valleys as well \nas the higher terrain due to a potential CAD as high pressure \nremains to our northeast. Impacts are currently expected to be\nminimal though and mainly confined to the higher terrain. East-\nnortheasterly winds will increase Thursday night into Friday\nalong coastal locations with gusts up to around 35 mph likely.\nFurther inland confidence in gusty winds is lower due to the CAD\nand associated inversion. \n\nTides remain astronomically high thru the weekend, with the highest \ntides occurring Thu afternoon. Meanwhile winds will veer around to \nnortheasterly by Wed and surge will begin to increase. Even without \nsurge tides each afternoon will be running near action stage, so \nhigh water levels around high tide are anticipated thru at least \nSat. The strongest winds will arrive late Thu into Fri, but at this \ntime are not expected to overlap with the higher of the two tides. \nHowever both Thu and Fri afternoons will only need about 0.5 ft of \nsurge to reach minor flooding and this seems fairly likely given the \nwind/wave forecast. At this time the lack of strong onshore \ncomponent and relatively modest wind speeds for a low pressure means \nthat moderate flood stage is unlikely, especially farther northeast \nalong the coast. Hampton Harbor will be most likely to come within a \nhalf foot of moderate flooding as winds will tend to more of an \nonshore component for the Seacoast.\n\nShowery and cool conditions will persist Friday night into\nSaturday as the H5 low meanders overhead. Cooler temperatures\nwill return aloft, allowing for some snow across the higher\nterrain. Another system is possible early next week but forecast\nconfidence is low at this time.",
